message: "If this is useful in your work, we would appreciate a citation."
type: software
title: "mojolearn: GPU machine-learning algorithms in Mojo, portable to Apple Silicon"
authors:
  - family-names: Hendel
    given-names: Andrew
    alias: ajhendel
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0000-9877-3623"
    affiliation: "Latent Archon"
license: Apache-2.0
version: "0.2.0"
date-released: "2026-08-28"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22068633
repository-code: "https://github.com/mojolearn/mojolearn"
abstract: >-
  Ports of GPU machine-learning algorithms into Mojo from the CUDA
  implementations that established them, targeting Apple Silicon via Metal
  first and CUDA and ROCm from the same source. Covers gradient-boosted
  oblivious (symmetric) trees from CatBoost; random forests, extremely
  randomized trees, DBSCAN, PCA, ridge and logistic regression, coordinate
  descent, support vector classification, kernel density estimation,
  single-linkage and spectral clustering, isolation forests, Holt-Winters
  and the scoring metrics from cuML; k-means and brute-force
  k-nearest-neighbors from cuVS; and ordinary least squares, top-k selection
  and the MST and Lanczos primitives from RAFT. The algorithms are the
  upstreams' work and every derivation is recorded per file in the PORTED_MAP
  tables; see NOTICE for the license obligations each carries, including the
  MIT-licensed FAISS warp-select queue that RAFT vendors. What is original is
  the host-side control plane, the determinism ladder that the CUDA upstreams
  have no counterpart for, and the deviations Metal forced.
keywords:
  - machine learning
  - GPU
  - Metal
  - Apple Silicon
  - Mojo
  - clustering
  - k-means
  - DBSCAN
  - nearest neighbors
  - PCA
  - gradient boosting
  - oblivious trees
